Linear drag, $bold(F) = -k_1 bold(v)$, models viscous effects where objects move the medium with them, such as a spoon in honey. For a spherical object of radius $L$, Stokes' law gives $k_1 = 6 ppi eta L$, where $eta$ is the viscosity of the medium.

Quadratic drag, $bold(F) = -k_2 abs(bold(v)) bold(v)$, arises when an object bumps into molecules: the collision rate is proportional to the speed $abs(bold(v))$, and each collision imparts a momentum change proportional to $abs(bold(v))$, so the force is proportional to $abs(bold(v))^2$. The number of collisions depends on the density of the medium $rho$ and the cross-sectional area $A$ of the object, so $k_2 prop rho A$. By dimensions,
$
  [F] = M L T^(-2), quad [k_2 v^2] = [k_2] (L T^(-1))^2 = [k_2] L^2 T^(-2) quad => quad [k_2] = M L^(-1) = [rho A].
$

Both linear and quadratic drag are typically present; $k_1$ and $k_2$ are called coefficients of friction.
